---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const principles = [
  { id: 'p1', n: '01', title: 'Systems &gt; Discipline' },
  { id: 'p2', n: '02', title: 'End every status report with three questions' },
  { id: 'p3', n: '03', title: 'Name the elephant, never the person' },
  { id: 'p4', n: '04', title: 'Put people where they&rsquo;re already motivated' },
  { id: 'p5', n: '05', title: 'Show people the level above their own' },
  { id: 'p6', n: '06', title: 'Make my team so elite they don&rsquo;t need me' },
];
---
<Base
  title="Philosophies · Josh Rutz"
  description="How I think and lead, written down — the principles I've actually used, not the ones that sound good in an interview."
>
  <header class="hero" id="top"><div class="wrap">
    <div class="eyebrow reveal">How I lead</div>
    <h1 class="reveal">My philosophies</h1>
    <p class="lede reveal">Notes to myself, written down so I stop trusting memory: the principles
      I&rsquo;ve actually used, not the ones that sound good in an interview. One idea sits under all of
      them. People have a limited daily budget of good decisions, and my job is to keep that budget off
      the boilerplate and spend it on the few decisions and conversations that genuinely need a human.</p>
  </div></header>

  <section><div class="wrap">
    <div class="label reveal" id="six">The six<span class="rule"></span></div>
    <div class="philo-grid">
      {principles.map((p) => (
        <a class="philo-card reveal" href={`#${p.id}`}>
          <span class="pc-fx"><span class="pc-drop"><span class="pc-lobe a"></span><span class="pc-lobe b"></span></span></span>
          <span class="pc-num">{p.n}</span>
          <span class="pc-title" set:html={p.title}></span>
          <span class="pc-read">Read</span>
        </a>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div></section>

  <section id="p1" class="philo-section"><div class="wrap">
    <div class="eyebrow reveal">Principle 01</div>
    <h2 class="reveal">Systems &gt; Discipline</h2>
    <p class="body reveal">If a team has to rely on willpower to do the right thing every time, I&rsquo;ve
      built the wrong system. Discipline runs out, and decision fatigue spends the same budget that hard
      problems need. So I&rsquo;d rather fix the environment once than ask people to be disciplined forever.</p>
    <p class="body reveal">A concrete one. Designers on my team wrote their own Lua to drive game and
      character logic. Smart people, but some of the code was getting out of hand: heavy loops, no review.
      I didn&rsquo;t ask them to write cleaner code. I changed the path instead. They keep writing Lua
      (autonomy), but a commit needs a programmer&rsquo;s sign-off (within scope). Inefficient loops dropped
      sharply, and the review surface kept catching spots where a single new C++ API call would make a whole
      class of Lua cheaper.</p>
    <p class="body reveal">The designers didn&rsquo;t love being slowed down. What sold it was the trade:
      in exchange for the gate, performance stopped being their problem and moved to the engineers.
      That&rsquo;s the rule under the rule. A good constraint does more than say no. It gives something back
      for what it takes.</p>
    <a class="backlink reveal" href="#six">Back to the six</a>
  </div></section>

  <section id="p2" class="philo-section"><div class="wrap">
    <div class="eyebrow reveal">Principle 02</div>
    <h2 class="reveal">End every status report with three questions</h2>
    <p class="body reveal">When someone finishes telling me where their work stands, I ask the same three
      things. Are you blocked? Are you delayed? Are you confused about the requirements?</p>
    <p class="body reveal">I want everyone to always have work they can move on, and those three questions
      cover the only reasons they can&rsquo;t. If the answer to all three is no, I stay out of the way. They
      have what they need, and stepping in would just be noise. If any one of them is yes, that&rsquo;s my
      cue: reprioritize something, set up the meeting, or clear up the requirement, so they can get back to
      their own work.</p>
    <p class="body reveal">The questions aren&rsquo;t a check on the person. They&rsquo;re a check on whether
      the system around the person is holding them up.</p>
    <a class="backlink reveal" href="#six">Back to the six</a>
  </div></section>

  <section id="p3" class="philo-section"><div class="wrap">
    <div class="eyebrow reveal">Principle 03</div>
    <h2 class="reveal">Name the elephant, never the person</h2>
    <p class="body reveal">Most work meetings I sit in could end early. When everyone&rsquo;s repeating what
      we all already know, no new information is moving, and the meeting is just heat. The useful moments are
      the hard ones: the blocker no one&rsquo;s saying, the miscommunication two people are quietly working
      around.</p>
    <p class="body reveal">So I bring it up. There&rsquo;s a method to it, and the method is the whole thing.</p>
    <p class="body reveal">It&rsquo;s us against the problem, never person against person. I state the problem
      the team is having and put everyone on the same side of it. Nobody&rsquo;s in the dock.</p>
    <p class="body reveal">Process problems get aired to the group. People problems never do. If the issue is
      a specific person rather than a situation, that&rsquo;s a 1:1, in private. Calling someone out in a room
      doesn&rsquo;t create progress, it creates a defended, shut-down room.</p>
    <p class="body reveal">I find the elephant by staying close to my team, my peers, and my boss, so I feel
      the tension when it shows up. Nine times out of ten it&rsquo;s a misunderstanding, not a real
      disagreement. Most engineers would rather avoid a direct confrontation than walk into one, so I stand
      in as the intermediary: I keep my own feelings out of it and let both sides air their context until the
      misunderstanding comes apart. That way nobody has to risk mixing how they feel with what&rsquo;s
      actually wrong.</p>
    <a class="backlink reveal" href="#six">Back to the six</a>
  </div></section>

  <section id="p4" class="philo-section"><div class="wrap">
    <div class="eyebrow reveal">Principle 04</div>
    <h2 class="reveal">Put people where they&rsquo;re already motivated</h2>
    <p class="body reveal">Three things drive good work: autonomy, mastery, and purpose. Two out of three
      gets someone engaged. All three gives you the best team you&rsquo;ll ever work on. They don&rsquo;t come
      from the same place, and treating them as one lever is the mistake.</p>
    <p class="body reveal"><strong>Purpose</strong> comes from the organization: the mission, the product,
      the reason any of this matters. I keep it visible. I don&rsquo;t invent a private version of it for each
      person.</p>
    <p class="body reveal"><strong>Mastery</strong> is personal and can&rsquo;t be assigned. It&rsquo;s the
      specific kind of work that clicks for someone and the area they want to grow in. The most I can do is
      expose people to enough different work that they find their thing, then stay out of their way while they
      chase it.</p>
    <p class="body reveal"><strong>Autonomy</strong> is the one I actually control, and it&rsquo;s a dial, not
      a switch. Grant none and I&rsquo;m a micromanager. Grant all of it and I&rsquo;m lazy, and the team
      drifts. What I aim for is autonomy within scope: enough room that people feel invested and can move,
      inside a boundary that keeps everyone pointed the same way. A boundary set right gives something back
      for what it takes.</p>
    <a class="backlink reveal" href="#six">Back to the six</a>
  </div></section>

  <section id="p5" class="philo-section"><div class="wrap">
    <div class="eyebrow reveal">Principle 05</div>
    <h2 class="reveal">Show people the level above their own</h2>
    <p class="body reveal">For someone to level up, they have to want to see the meaning one level above
      their work. For an IC, that&rsquo;s how their work affects the team. For a lead, how the team&rsquo;s
      work affects the studio. For a studio director, how the studio advances the organization&rsquo;s goals.</p>
    <p class="body reveal">That wider view does two things. It&rsquo;s how a person grows into the next level
      before they&rsquo;re in it. And it surfaces options that would otherwise go unseen: when a technical or
      artistic developer knows what a feature is actually for, they can propose alternatives only their
      discipline would think of. A cheaper path, a better effect, a different shape of the same outcome.</p>
    <p class="body reveal">The person closest to the craft often holds the best idea. They can only offer it
      once they can see the goal from above.</p>
    <a class="backlink reveal" href="#six">Back to the six</a>
  </div></section>

  <section id="p6" class="philo-section"><div class="wrap">
    <div class="eyebrow reveal">Principle 06</div>
    <h2 class="reveal">Make my team so elite they don&rsquo;t need me</h2>
    <p class="body reveal">I want everyone on my team to level up into high-level seniors who can run
      autonomously. That means every raise, promotion, and bonus I can advocate for, I do. I want them to
      have all of it.</p>
    <p class="body reveal">I&rsquo;m honest about the selfish half. A team of autonomous seniors is a team I
      barely have to maintain, and every hour I&rsquo;m not spending holding the existing system together is
      an hour I can put into making it better. The generous goal and the selfish one run the same direction:
      the more my people grow, the less my job is maintenance and the more it&rsquo;s improvement.</p>
    <a class="backlink reveal" href="#six">Back to the six</a>
  </div></section>
</Base>

<style>
  .philo-section { scroll-margin-top: 86px; }
  #six { scroll-margin-top: 86px; }

  .philo-grid {
    margin-top: 10px;
    display: grid;
    gap: 16px;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr));
  }

  /* neutral panel (depth from light) + a liquid green cursor-light */
  .philo-card {
    position: relative;
    display: flex; flex-direction: column;
    min-height: 152px;
    border-radius: 24px;
    background: var(--paper);
    padding: 22px 22px 18px;
    overflow: hidden;
    text-decoration: none; color: inherit;
    box-shadow: 16px 16px 44px var(--sh-dark), -13px -13px 36px var(--sh-light);
    transition: transform .3s var(--ease-out), box-shadow .4s var(--ease-out);
  }
  .pc-num { font-size: 11px; letter-spacing: .09em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--accent); font-weight: 600; }
  .pc-title { font-family: var(--display); font-weight: 700; font-size: 20px; line-height: 1.18; letter-spacing: -.01em; margin: 10px 0 0; color: var(--ink); }
  .pc-read { margin-top: auto; padding-top: 14px; font-size: 12.5px; color: var(--ink-faint);
    display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 6px; transition: color .25s var(--ease-out), gap .25s var(--ease-out); }
  .pc-read::after { content: "\2193"; }

  /* the liquid light, contained inside the panel so green never floods */
  .pc-fx { position: absolute; inset: 0; border-radius: inherit; overflow: hidden; pointer-events: none; z-index: 0; }
  .pc-drop {
    position: absolute; left: 0; top: 0; width: 260px; height: 260px; margin: -130px 0 0 -130px;
    transform: translate(var(--bx, 50%), var(--by, 50%));
    opacity: 0; transition: opacity .45s var(--ease-out);
  }
  .pc-lobe { position: absolute; inset: 0; background: var(--glow); filter: blur(22px);
    animation-play-state: paused; will-change: transform, border-radius; }
  .pc-lobe.a { border-radius: 46% 54% 57% 43% / 53% 46% 54% 47%; animation: pc-spinA 8s linear infinite, pc-morphA 6s ease-in-out infinite alternate; }
  .pc-lobe.b { border-radius: 54% 46% 43% 57% / 47% 54% 46% 53%; animation: pc-spinB 11s linear infinite, pc-morphB 7.5s ease-in-out infinite alternate; }
  .philo-card > :not(.pc-fx) { position: relative; z-index: 1; }

  @keyframes pc-spinA { to { transform: rotate(360deg); } }
  @keyframes pc-spinB { to { transform: rotate(-360deg); } }
  @keyframes pc-morphA {
    from { border-radius: 46% 54% 57% 43% / 53% 46% 54% 47%; }
    to   { border-radius: 62% 38% 47% 53% / 42% 60% 40% 58%; }
  }
  @keyframes pc-morphB {
    from { border-radius: 54% 46% 43% 57% / 47% 54% 46% 53%; }
    to   { border-radius: 38% 62% 56% 44% / 59% 41% 58% 42%; }
  }

  .backlink { display: inline-block; margin-top: 22px; font-size: 12.5px; letter-spacing: .04em;
    text-transform: uppercase; font-weight: 600; color: var(--ink-faint); text-decoration: none;
    transition: color .25s var(--ease-out); }
  .backlink::before { content: "\2191  "; }
  .backlink:hover { color: var(--accent); }

  @media (hover:hover) and (pointer:fine) {
    .philo-card:hover {
      transform: translateY(-4px);
      box-shadow: 20px 20px 56px var(--sh-dark), -15px -15px 42px var(--sh-light), 0 0 0 1px var(--glow);
    }
    .philo-card:hover .pc-drop { opacity: 1; }
    .philo-card:hover .pc-lobe { animation-play-state: running; }
    .philo-card:hover .pc-read { color: var(--accent); gap: 9px; }
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .pc-lobe { animation: none; }
    .philo-card:hover { transform: none; }
  }
</style>

<script>
  // The liquid cursor-light lags behind the pointer (exponential smoothing -> no overshoot / no bounce).
  const fine = matchMedia('(hover:hover) and (pointer:fine)').matches;
  if (fine) {
    const E = 0.18; // lower = laggier
    const states = [];
    for (const card of document.querySelectorAll('.philo-card')) {
      const st = { card, tx: 0, ty: 0, cx: 0, cy: 0, on: false, init: false };
      states.push(st);
      const aim = (e) => {
        const r = card.getBoundingClientRect();
        st.tx = e.clientX - r.left;
        st.ty = e.clientY - r.top;
      };
      card.addEventListener('pointerenter', (e) => {
        aim(e);
        if (!st.init) { st.cx = st.tx; st.cy = st.ty; st.init = true; }
        st.on = true; ensure();
      });
      card.addEventListener('pointerleave', () => { st.on = false; });
      card.addEventListener('pointermove', aim);
    }
    let running = false;
    function frame() {
      let active = false;
      for (const st of states) {
        st.cx += (st.tx - st.cx) * E;
        st.cy += (st.ty - st.cy) * E;
        st.card.style.setProperty('--bx', st.cx.toFixed(1) + 'px');
        st.card.style.setProperty('--by', st.cy.toFixed(1) + 'px');
        if (st.on || Math.abs(st.tx - st.cx) + Math.abs(st.ty - st.cy) > 0.5) active = true;
      }
      if (active) requestAnimationFrame(frame); else running = false;
    }
    function ensure() { if (!running) { running = true; requestAnimationFrame(frame); } }
  }
</script>
